# print(number(JSON.parse('5')), number(JSON.parse('9')));
# -- end of source
# `limit` comes out of JSON, so `i` is an integer of 53 bits by type and no int32. What is stored is boxed as an int32 where it fits. The store that falls out of line (the list is empty, its vector is
# full) once called the stub for numbers, whose operation makes a list of int32s one of doubles for good; every front that asks the list for int32s then failed at every entry. A type without a
# fraction calls the stub for values: the list follows what is really stored.
